bootforge/engineering-practice/practice/.solution:
export function migrate(db, migration) {
  if (db.prepare('SELECT id FROM _migrations WHERE id = ?').get(migration.id)) return false;
  db.exec('BEGIN');
  try {
    db.exec(migration.sql);
    db.prepare('INSERT INTO _migrations (id) VALUES (?)').run(migration.id);
    db.exec('COMMIT');
    return true;
  } catch (error) {
    db.exec('ROLLBACK');
    throw error;
  }
}

bootforge/engineering-practice/practice/.instructions:
Existing schema: users(id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, email TEXT NOT NULL), _migrations(id TEXT PRIMARY KEY).
Export migrateEmails(db). Migration id is '002-normalized-email'. Already recorded -> false.
Inside ONE transaction: reject duplicate lower(trim(email)) values with Error; add email_normalized TEXT;
backfill every row with lower(trim(email)); create UNIQUE INDEX users_email_normalized_unique ON users(email_normalized);
record the migration, commit, return true. On failure roll back and rethrow. Do not delete/merge rows or change email.
Inputs use ASCII emails. No open transaction exists when called. This backfill does not implement future application dual-writes;
those require a separate rollout step. The uniqueness index allows NULL, so application writes must supply normalized values.

