bootforge/engineering-practice/practice/.solution:
export function migrateEmails(db) {
  const id = '002-normalized-email';
  if (db.prepare('SELECT id FROM _migrations WHERE id = ?').get(id)) return false;
  db.exec('BEGIN');
  try {
    const duplicate = db.prepare('SELECT lower(trim(email)) AS normalized FROM users GROUP BY lower(trim(email)) HAVING COUNT(*) > 1 LIMIT 1').get();
    if (duplicate) throw new Error('duplicate normalized email');
    db.exec('ALTER TABLE users ADD COLUMN email_normalized TEXT');
    db.exec('UPDATE users SET email_normalized = lower(trim(email))');
    db.exec('CREATE UNIQUE INDEX users_email_normalized_unique ON users(email_normalized)');
    db.prepare('INSERT INTO _migrations(id) VALUES (?)').run(id);
    db.exec('COMMIT');
    return true;
  } catch (error) {
    db.exec('ROLLBACK');
    throw error;
  }
}

bootforge/engineering-practice/practice/.instructions:
You review PR #42. Auth middleware verifies a session and sets req.user={id,orgId}. A task belongs to exactly one org.
Product contract: members may edit title/done of tasks in their own org; callers cannot reassign ownership or identity.
Missing and inaccessible tasks both return 404. The repository supports findOne(where), updateOne(where,patch),
and returns null when no row matches. updateOne is an atomic scoped update.

Proposed handler (line numbers are part of the review):
1 app.patch('/tasks/:id', requireSession, async (req,res) => {
2   const task = await repo.findOne({id:req.params.id});
3   if (!task) return res.status(404).json({error:'not found'});
4   const updated = await repo.updateOne({id:task.id}, req.body);
5   return res.json(updated);
6 });

PR test: member in org A edits the title of a task in org A and gets 200.
Write a review with blocking findings, one concrete exploit scenario, corrected query/update boundaries,
a validation strategy, and at least four regression tests. State a merge recommendation.

Product contract: products are organization-owned; a member can order only from their org. Stock may never go below zero.
Creating the order and decrementing stock must succeed or fail together. req.user.orgId comes from a verified session.
Assume an async SQL client that supports db.transaction(async tx => ...), row counts, and parameterized SQL.

Proposed change:
1 const product = await db.one('SELECT * FROM products WHERE id = ?', [req.body.productId]);
2 if (product.stock < req.body.qty) return res.status(409).json({error:'out of stock'});
3 await db.run('UPDATE products SET stock = ? WHERE id = ?', [product.stock - req.body.qty, product.id]);
4 const order = await db.insert('orders', {org_id:req.body.orgId, product_id:product.id, qty:req.body.qty});
5 res.status(201).json(order);

Evidence: stock starts at 1. Requests A and B both read 1 before either line 3 runs; both request qty=1.
Another run crashes between lines 3 and 4. Tests cover one successful order only.
Write a prioritized review with the interleaving's concrete outcome, ownership corrections, an atomic stock strategy,
failure handling, quantity validation, and tests. Do not prescribe a particular ORM.
